            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "To see what events may be happening, I used `inotifywait` (available as of FreeBSD 15) as follows (using Fish as the shell):\n\n```fish\nwhile true\n  inotifywait -q .git\nend\n```\n\nThis produces the following output when I perform the sequence that triggers the problem:\n\n```\n.git/ CLOSE_NOWRITE,CLOSE,ISDIR \n.git/ OPEN HEAD\n.git/ OPEN HEAD\n.git/ OPEN HEAD\n.git/ OPEN HEAD\n.git/ OPEN config\n.git/ OPEN HEAD\n```\n\nThe sequence in question is:\n\n1. Open `nvim`\n2. Open a file\n3. Run `:Git status`\n\nIf instead of `:Git status` I run `git status` in a `:terminal` buffer, only a `.git/ OPEN HEAD` event is produced.\n\nConsidering no renames appear to be happening, I'm not sure what's going on here. If I patch the callback to just return if `filename` is `nil` then all seems to be working fine, but it effectively hides the problem rather than truly fixing it.\n\nI'm still very new to FreeBSD so I can't really judge as to what's going on here, but given FreeBSD's small userbase (and thus getting less attention/care from projects) it may very well be a bug in either the luv bindings or uv itself.",
